---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CompressImage from '@/components/tools/image-edit/CompressImage.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="compress-image">
  <CompressImage client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to compress an image</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop in one or more photos. Nothing is uploaded; the encoder runs on your CPU.</li>
      <li>Pick a mode. <strong>By quality</strong> gives a 1–100 dial. <strong>By target file size</strong> lets you enter “200 KB”, and the tool finds the quality.</li>
      <li>Optionally limit the width. For a web page, this usually saves the most.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Compress</strong>, then drag the divider in the before/after view to check the result at full size before you download.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>How the target-size search works</h2>
    <p>
      No formula converts “quality 74” into “183 KB”: the size depends on the content of the image. Flat
      graphics compress very well; foliage, water and film grain hardly compress. So the tool measures.
      It encodes at full quality first, and stops if that fits. If not, it runs a binary search: encode
      at the midpoint of the range, keep the result and raise the floor if it fits, or lower the
      ceiling if it does not. Ten rounds narrow the quality range to about a tenth of a percent, much
      finer than the eye can see, and you get the highest result that fits.
    </p>
    <p>
      PNG is lossless, so it has no quality parameter to search. In target-size mode, the search uses the
      scale factor and reduces the pixel dimensions until the file fits. For photographs, JPEG or WebP
      output almost always looks better.
    </p>

    <h2>Quality settings, and what they actually cost</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Quality</th><th>Typical size vs original</th><th>What you see</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>95–100</td><td>60–90%</td><td>Visually lossless; wasteful for the web</td></tr>
          <tr><td>85–94</td><td>25–45%</td><td>No visible artefacts even at pixel level</td></tr>
          <tr><td>75–84</td><td>12–25%</td><td>The web sweet spot: no visible difference at normal size</td></tr>
          <tr><td>60–74</td><td>8–15%</td><td>Slight softening in fine texture; fine for thumbnails</td></tr>
          <tr><td>40–59</td><td>5–10%</td><td>Visible ringing on hard edges, banding in skies</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Below 40</td><td>3–6%</td><td>Obvious 8 × 8 blocking; only for placeholders</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Choosing a format</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Format</th><th>Best for</th><th>Transparency</th><th>Relative size</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>JPEG</td><td>Photographs, anything with smooth gradients</td><td>No</td><td>Baseline</td></tr>
          <tr><td>WebP</td><td>Almost everything on the modern web</td><td>Yes</td><td>25–35% smaller than JPEG</td></tr>
          <tr><td>PNG</td><td>Screenshots, logos, flat colour, sharp text</td><td>Yes</td><td>Much larger for photos</td></tr>
          <tr><td>AVIF</td><td>Maximum compression when encode time does not matter</td><td>Yes</td><td>Up to 50% smaller than JPEG</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Getting the most out of a photo budget</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Resize before you compress. A 4000 px photo shown in an 800 px column has 25× more pixels than it needs. Limit the width first; the quality dial then has much less work.</li>
      <li>Remove the metadata. EXIF blocks, embedded colour profiles and camera thumbnails can add 20–80 KB to each file. The <a href="/remove-exif-data">EXIF remover</a> clears them, and this tool also drops them when it re-encodes.</li>
      <li>Judge at 100%. The comparison slider shows both images at the same on-screen size. Zoom the page to 100% before you decide if an artefact matters.</li>
      <li>Never compress a compressed file again. Each JPEG generation adds to the errors of the previous one. Always start from the highest-quality original you have.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Repeated compression and why it degrades</h2>
    <p>
      JPEG splits the image into 8 × 8 blocks, converts each to frequency coefficients and quantises
      them. Each decode and re-encode quantises coefficients that were already rounded, so errors add
      up: edges get halos, flat areas get blotches, and colour bleeds across block boundaries. The same
      JPEG saved ten times at quality 80 looks much worse than the original saved once at quality 40.
      Keep your originals.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
